# Deploy

Two long-lived environments, each on its own VM (EC2, `us-east-2`), orchestrated by
[Dokploy](https://dokploy.com) on top of Docker Swarm. Web traffic enters through
Cloudflare; TLS at the origin is issued by the Traefik that Dokploy manages.

The local environment uses none of this — see [`local-environment.md`](local-environment.md).

## Topology

| | Staging | Production |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Front | `dev.{{ app_domain }}` | `{{ app_domain }}` |
| API | `dev.api.{{ app_domain }}` | `api.{{ app_domain }}` |
| Dokploy panel | `dev.deploy.{{ app_domain }}` | `deploy.{{ app_domain }}` |

Each VM runs its own Dokploy, its own Postgres, its own Redis and its own tunnel to the
legacy MySQL. **No resource is shared between the environments** — not the database, not
the panel, not the GitHub App.

The legacy MySQL lives on neither VM: a `legacy-tunnel` container (a `raw` compose in
Dokploy) keeps an SSH tunnel to the legacy host and publishes it on the `dokploy-network`
network under that name. That is why `MYSQL_LEGACY_DATABASE` points at
`legacy-tunnel:3306` and not at an IP.

## What gets built

Two images, built on the VM itself from the repository.

**`apps/api/Dockerfile`** — multi-stage on `node:22-slim`: `turbo prune` isolates the
`api` subgraph, compiles, installs production dependencies only and copies `dist` into
the runner. Exposes port **3000**, enters through `tini` → `docker-entrypoint.sh` and runs
`node dist/main`.

{% if web_stack == 'next' %}
**`apps/web/Dockerfile`** — multi-stage on `node:22-alpine`: `turbo prune web --docker`
isolates the front subgraph, installs, compiles with `next build` (`output: "standalone"`)
and copies `.next/standalone`, `.next/static` and `public` into the runner, which runs as
the non-root `nextjs` user. Exposes port **3000**, with no nginx — the standalone's own
`server.js` serves assets and routes: `CMD ["node", "apps/web/server.js"]`.

> **`NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` is a build arg, not a runtime variable.** The value is baked into
> the bundle during the Next build (`ARG` → `ENV` so Next can see it and so BuildKit
> invalidates the cache when the URL changes). Changing the API URL requires a **front
> rebuild** — changing the variable and restarting the container has no effect whatsoever.
{% else %}
**`apps/web/Dockerfile`** — compiles with Vite and serves the static output on
`nginx-unprivileged`, port **8080**, with a healthcheck at `/healthz`.

> **`VITE_API_URL` is a build arg, not a runtime variable.** The value is baked into the
> bundle during the build (`ARG` → `ENV` so Vite can see it and so BuildKit invalidates
> the cache when the URL changes). Changing the API URL requires a **front rebuild** —
> changing the variable and restarting the container has no effect whatsoever.
{% endif %}

## What runs at API boot

`docker-entrypoint.sh` runs three conditional steps before bringing the app up:

| Step | Condition | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Migrations (`node dist/db/migrate`) | `RUN_MIGRATIONS != false` | **on** |
| Superuser bootstrap (`node dist/seeds/bootstrap-master`) | `MASTER_EMAIL` **and** `MASTER_PASSWORD` set | conditional |
| Legacy backfill (`node dist/legacy-import/run`) | `RUN_BACKFILL == true` | **off** |

Migrations are fail-fast: a wrong schema aborts the boot. `RUN_MIGRATIONS=false` only
makes sense when a dedicated job migrates and the replicas only serve.

The bootstrap is idempotent (`onConflictDoNothing` by email), safe on every boot and per
replica. Without the secrets it becomes a no-op — the dev seed never runs here (`NODE_ENV`
guard).

The backfill is self-guarded (`_sync.states` ledger + advisory lock), so re-running it is
a no-op. Even so, the **default in a long-lived environment is to leave it off** and fire
it as a one-off deploy job:

```bash
docker run --rm <api-image> node dist/legacy-import/run
```

Turning on `RUN_BACKFILL=true` in an environment with a large database makes the API
unavailable for the whole import on every restart — the initial production load took
~19 min.

## Variables per environment

What differs between staging and production, and must never be copied from one to the
other:

| Variable | Note |
| --- | --- |
| `DATABASE_URL`, `REDIS_URL` | point at the environment's own services (Swarm service name, not an IP) |
| `WEB_ORIGIN` | the front's exact origin; CORS mirrors only that one |
| `VITE_API_URL` (front) | build arg — see the warning above |
| `PASSWORD_PEPPER`, `CSRF_SECRET` | **generate new ones per environment**; never reuse staging's |
| `MASTER_EMAIL`, `MASTER_PASSWORD` | initial superuser credential |
| `MYSQL_LEGACY_DATABASE` | connection via `legacy-tunnel` |
| `DATABASE_POOL_MAX` | ceiling of the application pool's connections; saturated → `PoolSaturatedError`, **503** with `Retry-After` (ADR 0089) |
| `DATABASE_POOL_MAX_WAITING` | ceiling of the wait queue for a connection; above it, acquisition is refused immediately (same 503) instead of queueing |
| `DATABASE_IDLE_IN_TX_TIMEOUT_MS` | ceiling for an open, idle transaction; above it Postgres terminates the session |

The rest (session parameters, Argon2, lockout, cookie, timeouts) is pure configuration and
can be identical in both. Validation is fail-fast per module: a missing value or wrong
format brings the boot down with an explicit error.

## Deploy flow

1. Push to `main` on `{{ github_org }}/{{ github_repo }}`.
2. The GitHub App installed in each environment fires that VM's Dokploy webhook.
3. Dokploy runs `git fetch`, builds the image on the VM and updates the service in the Swarm.
4. Traefik reconfigures the route; the origin certificate already exists (Let's Encrypt).

Auto-deploy on push is on in both environments, pointing at the **same branch** (`main`).
Each VM has its own GitHub App — connecting one does not connect the other.

The quality gate runs **before** that: lefthook on pre-push (typecheck + unit) and CI on
PR/push (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`). The Dokploy build runs no tests.

## Known pitfalls

**Never deploy a long-lived environment with `Dockerfile.dev`.** `docker-entrypoint.dev.sh`
applies migrations and backfill, but does **not** run `bootstrap-master` — the environment
comes up with zero users and nobody can authenticate. On top of that it runs `pnpm dev`
(ts-node in watch mode) instead of the compiled build. `.dev` exists for local
development, not for staging or production.

**Migrations can be silently skipped on a long-lived database.** Drizzle's migrator only
runs entries whose timestamp is greater than the last applied one. If a migration lands on
`main` with a timestamp earlier than what the environment has already applied — which
happens when parallel branches create migrations with the same number and get merged out
of order — it is ignored on every boot, forever, and a redeploy does not fix it.

Before assuming an environment is up to date, compare against the journal:

```sql
SELECT count(*) FROM drizzle.__drizzle_migrations;
```

The total must match the number of entries in
`apps/api/drizzle/migrations/meta/_journal.json`. If it differs, the database has drift —
and a staging database with drift **is not a valid source for copying data** into
production.

**The build consumes the VM.** The build happens on the very machine that serves the
environment; a heavy build competes with the running application. Both environments run
with swap configured and a daily Docker cache cleanup (cron at 04:00) precisely for that
reason.

## Operations

Access to the VMs is by SSH with a key, entering through the EC2 Instance Connect Endpoint
— there is no per-IP allowance, and without an active SSO session there is no SSH. The
Postgres port is closed in both environments. The path to reach the database depends on
the environment and is detailed in [`../agents/infra.md`](../agents/infra.md): on staging
the `ssh -L` tunnel reaches it, on production it does not — Postgres exists only on the
Swarm overlay network.

Useful commands on the VM:

```bash
docker service ls                          # state of the Swarm services
docker service logs --tail 50 <service>    # logs of one service
docker system df                           # Docker disk usage
```

Migrations, bootstrap and backfill can also be fired by hand from the already-built image,
without a redeploy:

```bash
docker run --rm <api-image> node dist/db/migrate
docker run --rm <api-image> node dist/seeds/bootstrap-master
```
